{"name":"Tropical Rainforest","hex":"#00755e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tropical rainforest","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A forest with high rate of precipitation and high humidity, usually located near the equator."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tropical_rainforest"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tropical rainforest","blocks":[{"text":"Tropical rainforests are dense and warm rainforests with high rainfall typically found between 10° north and south of the Equator. They are a subset of the tropical forest biome that occurs roughly within the 28° latitudes. Tropical rainforests are a type of tropical moist broadleaf forest, that includes the more extensive seasonal tropical forests. True rainforests usually occur in tropical rainforest climates where no dry season occurs; all months have an average precipitation of at least 60 mm (2.4 in). Seasonal tropical forests with tropical monsoon or savanna climates are sometimes included in the broader definition."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_rainforest","wikidata":"Q199403"},{"title":"Tropics","blocks":[{"text":"The tropics are the region of Earth surrounding the equator, where the Sun may shine directly overhead. This contrasts with the temperate or polar regions of Earth, where the Sun can never be directly overhead. Because of Earth's axial tilt, the width of the tropics is twice the tilt. The tropics are also referred to as the tropical zone and the torrid zone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png/330px-World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropics","wikidata":"Q42530"},{"title":"Rainforest","blocks":[{"text":"Rainforests are forests characterized by a closed and continuous tree canopy, moisture-dependent vegetation, the presence of epiphytes and lianas and the absence of wildfire. Rainforests can generally be classified as tropical rainforests or temperate rainforests, but other types have been described."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Chiapas_Rainforest_crop.jpg/330px-Chiapas_Rainforest_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainforest","wikidata":"Q9444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Tropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tropical forests: from the UN FRA2000 report","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATropical_Forests_2000_by_Major_Ecological_Domains.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Rio_Madre_de_Dios%2C_Peru.JPG/500px-Rio_Madre_de_Dios%2C_Peru.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Rio_Madre_de_Dios%2C_Peru.JPG/1280px-Rio_Madre_de_Dios%2C_Peru.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_